1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

﻿1
00:00:03,703 --> 00:00:04,938
And turnover!

2
00:00:04,938 --> 00:00:06,539
I'm Alastair Bruce,

3
00:00:06,539 --> 00:00:09,009
on the set of Downton Abbey!

4
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:10,744
As the show's
 historical advisor,

5
00:00:10,744 --> 00:00:13,079
I help cast and crew
 create a world

6
00:00:13,079 --> 00:00:15,048
that is true
 to Britain's past.

7
00:00:15,048 --> 00:00:16,549
MICHELLE DOCKERY:
 I said to Alistair,

8
00:00:16,549 --> 00:00:18,151
"Can I lean back
 just a little bit?"

9
00:00:18,151 --> 00:00:19,352
And he says, "Absolutely not,

10
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:21,287
not in front
 of your grandmother."

11
00:00:21,287 --> 00:00:24,124
Join me for "The Manners
 of Downton Abbey,"

12
00:00:24,124 --> 00:00:26,226
a Masterpiece special.

13
00:00:26,226 --> 00:00:28,428
♫♫

14
00:00:39,973 --> 00:00:42,075
♫♫

15
00:00:46,012 --> 00:00:49,315
ALASTAIR BRUCE:
 The lives of the people
 in Downton Abbey

16
00:00:49,315 --> 00:00:51,384
are a glory of ritual
 and grandeur.

17
00:00:51,384 --> 00:00:53,953
ROBERT:
 Welcome to Downton.

18
00:00:53,953 --> 00:00:57,490
BRUCE:
 A ballet of strange manners
 and stiff formality.

19
00:00:57,490 --> 00:00:58,825
ROBERT:
 I nearly came down

20
00:00:58,825 --> 00:00:59,859
in a dinner jacket tonight.

21
00:00:59,859 --> 00:01:00,860
Oh, really?

22
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:02,162
Well, why not
 a dressing gown,

23
00:01:02,162 --> 00:01:04,197
or better still, pajamas?

24
00:01:04,197 --> 00:01:07,133
BRUCE:
 The aristocrats of Edwardian
 England and their servants

25
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:09,702
lived by a very odd
 set of rules,

26
00:01:09,702 --> 00:01:13,606
a world away
 from the way we behave today.

27
00:01:13,606 --> 00:01:18,378
But to them, these manners were
 the way they lived their life,

28
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:20,246
and what a life!

29
00:01:26,319 --> 00:01:28,521
Manners came into everything--

30
00:01:28,521 --> 00:01:32,725
how you dressed, how you ate,
 how you stood, how you spoke.

31
00:01:32,725 --> 00:01:35,095
Every detail mattered.

32
00:01:35,095 --> 00:01:38,131
It said who you were
 and where you belonged.

33
00:01:38,131 --> 00:01:40,200
Were you from above stairs
 or below?

34
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,169
You are a footman,
 and a footman wears gloves.

35
00:01:43,169 --> 00:01:45,972
So if we could begin.

36
00:01:45,972 --> 00:01:49,542
These were the rules for life
 at the time of Downton Abbey.

37
00:01:49,542 --> 00:01:51,277
They were a secret code

38
00:01:51,277 --> 00:01:54,347
that tells you everything
 about Edwardian Britain.

39
00:01:54,347 --> 00:01:57,584
And I'm going to let you in
 on that secret.

40
00:02:01,221 --> 00:02:03,189
I'm Alastair Bruce,
 historian,

41
00:02:03,189 --> 00:02:06,059
expert on British
 royal ceremony,

42
00:02:06,059 --> 00:02:07,460
and historical advisor

43
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:11,598
to cast and crew
 on set at Downton Abbey.

44
00:02:11,598 --> 00:02:12,632
Just in the background.

45
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:15,235
Alastair's referred to
 as the Oracle.

46
00:02:15,235 --> 00:02:17,103
BRENDAN COYLE:
 Couldn't work without Alastair.

47
00:02:17,103 --> 00:02:18,338
He just knows everything.

48
00:02:18,338 --> 00:02:21,374
You give an indication to them
 to come through.

49
00:02:21,374 --> 00:02:23,476
He taught me how to sit
 and stand and walk and talk.

50
00:02:23,476 --> 00:02:24,777
That's just perfect.

51
00:02:24,777 --> 00:02:27,046
MICHELLE DOCKERY:
 I've sometimes said to Alastair,

52
00:02:27,046 --> 00:02:28,615
"Can I lean back
 just a little bit?"

53
00:02:28,615 --> 00:02:29,816
And he says,
 "Absolutely not,

54
00:02:29,816 --> 00:02:32,051
not in front
 of your grandmother."

55
00:02:32,051 --> 00:02:34,521
CREW:
 Here we go for rehearsal,
 please, nice and quiet.

56
00:02:34,521 --> 00:02:35,588
You know you've got
 a military cross?

57
00:02:35,588 --> 00:02:36,589
I didn't.

58
00:02:36,589 --> 00:02:38,525
Which means he was pretty brave
 in the trenches.

59
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:41,928
It's a constant struggle,
 which I adore,

60
00:02:41,928 --> 00:02:44,230
but it's so difficult
 to get people back in time,

61
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:46,499
to when rules were so different.

62
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:49,135
LILY JAMES:
 The body language
 was very restrained.

63
00:02:49,135 --> 00:02:51,471
It's not polite
 to sort of slouch

64
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:53,940
or, you know,
 put your hands all over,

65
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:55,508
you know, move like we do
 in a modern way.

66
00:02:55,508 --> 00:02:57,977
KEVIN DOYLE:
 I hadn't realized that

67
00:02:57,977 --> 00:02:59,646
there was also
 a hierarchy downstairs.

68
00:02:59,646 --> 00:03:01,548
ED SPELEERS:
 It was a very dog-eat-dog world

69
00:03:01,548 --> 00:03:02,849
amongst the staff.

70
00:03:05,418 --> 00:03:06,753
And this coat's all right?

71
00:03:06,753 --> 00:03:08,721
This coat's perfect as long as
 you have that popper undone.

72
00:03:08,721 --> 00:03:09,889
(laughing)

73
00:03:12,892 --> 00:03:15,061
BRUCE:
 I want to give you
 my insider's guide

74
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:17,363
to the rules of living
 at Downton Abbey.

75
00:03:17,363 --> 00:03:18,364
CREW:
 And turnover.

76
00:03:18,364 --> 00:03:20,533
Turning, ready.

77
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:21,768
Action!

78
00:03:21,768 --> 00:03:23,803
♫♫

79
00:03:31,477 --> 00:03:32,946
Oh, there's poor
 Lady Raven.

80
00:03:32,946 --> 00:03:35,181
I really ought to go
 and talk to her.

81
00:03:35,181 --> 00:03:36,649
Of course,
 Your Grace.

82
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,122
Don't call her
 "Your Grace."

83
00:03:43,122 --> 00:03:44,490
TOM:
 I thought it was correct.

84
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:46,259
Call her "Duchess."

85
00:03:46,259 --> 00:03:47,360
But why?

86
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:48,595
I don't call you
 "Countess."

87
00:03:48,595 --> 00:03:50,029
Certainly not!

88
00:03:50,029 --> 00:03:51,397
But there's
 no logic in it.

89
00:03:51,397 --> 00:03:53,833
Oh no, if I were
 to search for logic,

90
00:03:53,833 --> 00:03:57,604
I should not look for it
 among the English upper class.

91
00:04:02,675 --> 00:04:04,844
Welcome to the library
 of Downton Abbey,

92
00:04:04,844 --> 00:04:07,647
which is not quite
 as you normally see it.

93
00:04:07,647 --> 00:04:09,482
The manners of Downton

94
00:04:09,482 --> 00:04:11,918
are not just about
 how to address a duchess

95
00:04:11,918 --> 00:04:13,786
or which knife and fork
 to pick up.

96
00:04:13,786 --> 00:04:16,155
They're much more important
 than that.

97
00:04:16,155 --> 00:04:18,625
It's key to how
 these people lived

98
00:04:18,625 --> 00:04:20,560
and exactly
 what they believed in.

99
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:22,262
As it is, my lord,

100
00:04:22,262 --> 00:04:25,431
we may have to have a maid
 in the dining room.

101
00:04:25,431 --> 00:04:26,733
Cheer up, Carson.

102
00:04:26,733 --> 00:04:28,468
There are worse things
 happening in the world.

103
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:31,404
Not worse than a maid
 serving a duke.

104
00:04:31,404 --> 00:04:34,674
BRUCE:
 Edwardian England was a time
 of great change.

105
00:04:34,674 --> 00:04:37,777
Both the Industrial Revolution
 and the Great War

106
00:04:37,777 --> 00:04:39,412
had altered everything.

107
00:04:39,412 --> 00:04:44,017
Is this an instrument
 of communication or torture?

108
00:04:44,017 --> 00:04:47,787
It seemed that centuries of
 tradition were all passing away.

109
00:04:47,787 --> 00:04:49,722
And for the aristocracy,

110
00:04:49,722 --> 00:04:52,058
they felt that they would lose
 all of it.

111
00:04:52,058 --> 00:04:54,927
Now we're to be turned
 out of Downton!

112
00:04:54,927 --> 00:05:00,199
Whether it was who to marry,
 how to eat or what to wear,

113
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:03,236
their manners held the old order
 in place

114
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:05,805
when everything else
 seemed to be falling apart.

115
00:05:05,805 --> 00:05:08,107
But why do the rituals,
 the clothes and the customs

116
00:05:08,107 --> 00:05:09,575
matter so much?

117
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:10,677
Because without them,

118
00:05:10,677 --> 00:05:12,679
we would be like
 the wild men of Borneo.

119
00:05:15,381 --> 00:05:18,618
BRUCE:
 And the best place to start
 understanding Edwardian manners

120
00:05:18,618 --> 00:05:21,321
is in the dining room.

121
00:05:21,321 --> 00:05:23,022
Dinner is served,
 Your Ladyship.

122
00:05:29,862 --> 00:05:32,832
The Edwardians liked to eat
 sumptuously.

123
00:05:32,832 --> 00:05:34,801
Showing off was a part of it.

124
00:05:34,801 --> 00:05:38,104
But it was not all about
 the glorious food.

125
00:05:38,104 --> 00:05:40,206
The ritual of the dining room

126
00:05:40,206 --> 00:05:42,909
was at the center
 of their lives.

127
00:05:42,909 --> 00:05:45,611
Well, I think there are far more
 important things to worry about

128
00:05:45,611 --> 00:05:47,080
than whether or not Carson minds
 serving cocktails.

129
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:48,648
BRUCE:
 My job is to ensure that

130
00:05:48,648 --> 00:05:51,617
that ritual
 is historically correct.

131
00:05:51,617 --> 00:05:54,420
I'm sorry,
 would you excuse me, Mama?

132
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,855
I've got rather a headache.

133
00:05:58,591 --> 00:06:00,460
CREW:
 Cut there, very good.

134
00:06:03,963 --> 00:06:06,232
Laura,
 can I talk to you?

135
00:06:06,232 --> 00:06:07,700
Where are your gloves?

136
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:09,135
They're on my lap,
 I'm taking them with me.

137
00:06:09,135 --> 00:06:10,503
Perfect, thanks
 very much indeed.

138
00:06:10,503 --> 00:06:11,804
Perfect, thanks.

139
00:06:11,804 --> 00:06:13,606
LAURA CARMICHAEL:
 You're sitting up straight,

140
00:06:13,606 --> 00:06:15,341
your evening gloves
 are on your lap,

141
00:06:15,341 --> 00:06:17,110
hidden underneath your napkin.

142
00:06:17,110 --> 00:06:19,946
Um, you don't rest your hands
 on the table,

143
00:06:19,946 --> 00:06:23,149
you have to remember
 which course you're on

144
00:06:23,149 --> 00:06:24,550
for which wine.

145
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:26,719
JIM CARTER:
 Red, water, white,
 is that right?

146
00:06:26,719 --> 00:06:29,055
BRUCE:
 Red, water...
 absolutely, yes.

147
00:06:29,055 --> 00:06:31,157
Yeah, yeah,
 that's the white one.

148
00:06:31,157 --> 00:06:32,725
JAMES:
 We always have three glasses,

149
00:06:32,725 --> 00:06:35,228
and there's the larger one
 for the red,

150
00:06:35,228 --> 00:06:36,729
slightly smaller for water,

151
00:06:36,729 --> 00:06:38,731
and then even smaller
 for the white wine.

152
00:06:38,731 --> 00:06:42,268
The main thing I had to learn
 I think was posture,

153
00:06:42,268 --> 00:06:44,670
was this idea
 of sitting up straight.

154
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:46,105
Like this?

155
00:06:46,105 --> 00:06:48,107
That's perfect,
 absolutely bolt upright.

156
00:06:48,107 --> 00:06:50,276
Thanks very much.

157
00:06:50,276 --> 00:06:51,911
The back of the chair was never
 for anyone to lean back on,

158
00:06:51,911 --> 00:06:52,912
as they do now.

159
00:06:52,912 --> 00:06:54,414
That's purely decorative,

160
00:06:54,414 --> 00:06:57,617
and it's purely so that the
 footman has something to hold.

161
00:06:57,617 --> 00:07:00,520
Nannies used to put knives here,

162
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:02,989
to make children
 sit up straight.

163
00:07:02,989 --> 00:07:06,225
Understanding your cutlery
 was essential.

164
00:07:06,225 --> 00:07:08,094
Failure to know your knives

165
00:07:08,094 --> 00:07:11,230
might lead
 to an embarrassing moment.

166
00:07:11,230 --> 00:07:13,499
JAMES:
 I've learnt from Alastair/
 Pretty Woman,

167
00:07:13,499 --> 00:07:16,636
you start from the outside
 and work your way in.

168
00:07:16,636 --> 00:07:18,237
Or it could be you start
 from the inside

169
00:07:18,237 --> 00:07:19,839
and work your way out.

170
00:07:19,839 --> 00:07:22,508
Either way, it's one of the two!

171
00:07:22,508 --> 00:07:23,943
Go on, then.

172
00:07:23,943 --> 00:07:28,948
Teaspoon, egg spoon,
 melon spoon, grapefruit spoon,

173
00:07:28,948 --> 00:07:30,550
jam spoon...

174
00:07:32,952 --> 00:07:34,520
Shall I tell you?

175
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:35,555
All right.

176
00:07:36,722 --> 00:07:39,725
A bouillon spoon.

177
00:07:39,725 --> 00:07:42,094
But I thought soup spoons
 were the same as tablespoons.

178
00:07:42,094 --> 00:07:44,464
Ah, so they are,
 but not for bouillon,

179
00:07:44,464 --> 00:07:47,233
which is drunk
 from a smaller dish.

180
00:07:47,233 --> 00:07:49,001
ROSAMUND:
 Oh, she's just very tired.

181
00:07:49,001 --> 00:07:50,670
She'll be fit as a flea
 tomorrow.

182
00:07:50,670 --> 00:07:51,671
CREW:
 Cut, very good, thank you.

183
00:07:51,671 --> 00:07:52,705
Okay, cut.

184
00:07:54,774 --> 00:07:57,176
The reason we take such trouble
 with the dining room scenes

185
00:07:57,176 --> 00:07:58,945
is that they say grace
 at the beginning,

186
00:07:58,945 --> 00:08:01,481
and that makes it
 the Lord's table.

187
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:05,218
And all the detail and sumptuous
 display and the manners

188
00:08:05,218 --> 00:08:07,987
reflect the struggle
 that they all have

189
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:11,157
to achieve a similarly perfect
 moral approach to life.

190
00:08:11,157 --> 00:08:15,761
The immaculate presentation
 was a statement

191
00:08:15,761 --> 00:08:17,463
of moral correctness to all.

192
00:08:22,768 --> 00:08:26,239
This is Basildon Park
 in Berkshire.

193
00:08:26,239 --> 00:08:30,176
It doubles as the Crawleys'
 London residence.

194
00:08:30,176 --> 00:08:33,045
Tomorrow, we're shooting
 a dinner scene,

195
00:08:33,045 --> 00:08:37,049
and the art department
 spare no effort to lay the table

196
00:08:37,049 --> 00:08:40,653
exactly as it would have been
 90 years ago.

197
00:08:42,822 --> 00:08:45,658
The Edwardians
 prized complexity.

198
00:08:45,658 --> 00:08:48,294
Perfection takes many hours,

199
00:08:48,294 --> 00:08:50,930
as it did
 for the Edwardian servants.

200
00:09:04,343 --> 00:09:06,112
Small fork, big fork,
 small fork.

201
00:09:06,112 --> 00:09:08,948
And then in a perfect world,
 on the other side,

202
00:09:08,948 --> 00:09:11,150
we'd have that size knife,
 that size knife,

203
00:09:11,150 --> 00:09:14,720
a not that size knife,
 and a spoon.

204
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,455
WOMAN:
 Okay, let's do that then.

205
00:09:16,455 --> 00:09:18,224
When the table
 is being set out,

206
00:09:18,224 --> 00:09:20,026
things have to be measured,

207
00:09:20,026 --> 00:09:22,562
so Carson has a rod
 with measurements on it,

208
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:26,699
and that determines
 the place settings

209
00:09:26,699 --> 00:09:28,267
between each place and the next,

210
00:09:28,267 --> 00:09:29,936
between the chair
 and the plate,

211
00:09:29,936 --> 00:09:31,704
between the plate
 and the glasses.

212
00:09:31,704 --> 00:09:33,372
So everything
 is measured out precisely.

213
00:09:33,372 --> 00:09:37,176
Each butler would have
 a measuring stick

214
00:09:37,176 --> 00:09:40,947
to mark exactly where
 the table settings were

215
00:09:40,947 --> 00:09:42,448
in relation to each other.

216
00:09:42,448 --> 00:09:45,284
So you'd start off
 by just taking the first mark

217
00:09:45,284 --> 00:09:49,922
and moving in the edge
 of the first knife.

218
00:09:49,922 --> 00:09:53,492
And then you'd be able
 to bring all the others in

219
00:09:53,492 --> 00:09:56,562
and then just bring it
 straight up to that line.

220
00:09:56,562 --> 00:10:01,367
They didn't use their fingers
 without gloves on

221
00:10:01,367 --> 00:10:04,437
because otherwise it would have
 left a mark on the silver.

222
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:06,405
And on the measuring points,

223
00:10:06,405 --> 00:10:09,842
they should be
 absolutely equidistant.

224
00:10:09,842 --> 00:10:12,111
Once you follow the line up
 through the spoon,

225
00:10:12,111 --> 00:10:13,946
through the first,

226
00:10:13,946 --> 00:10:16,282
and then that's just
 slightly off to the left,

227
00:10:16,282 --> 00:10:18,117
that's slightly off
 to the right,

228
00:10:18,117 --> 00:10:20,186
and that sits in there

229
00:10:20,186 --> 00:10:21,954
and it's also in direct line
 with that,

230
00:10:21,954 --> 00:10:23,856
you've got a place setting.

231
00:10:23,856 --> 00:10:26,492
And when it's all perfect,
 the last thing they did

232
00:10:26,492 --> 00:10:28,160
was take a certain length--
 I don't know,

233
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:30,463
it would be marked,
 but this has just been made up.

234
00:10:30,463 --> 00:10:31,764
Yeah, yeah.

235
00:10:31,764 --> 00:10:34,634
And then they would
 come right down.

236
00:10:34,634 --> 00:10:37,269
You set that against that
 and bring the chair out.

237
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:45,077
And then at the end of it,
 you just kneel down like that

238
00:10:45,077 --> 00:10:46,245
and you see a straight line.

239
00:10:46,245 --> 00:10:47,346
What do you think,
 it's not bad?

240
00:10:47,346 --> 00:10:48,347
No, that's good.

241
00:10:48,347 --> 00:10:49,382
Perfect.

242
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:56,455
What do you think?

243
00:10:56,455 --> 00:10:59,825
Nothing succeeds
 like excess.

244
00:10:59,825 --> 00:11:01,727
BRUCE:
 For the Edwardians,

245
00:11:01,727 --> 00:11:06,699
the point of a sumptuous table
 was to impress their guests.

246
00:11:06,699 --> 00:11:10,369
Downstairs, who served what
 was also a reflection

247
00:11:10,369 --> 00:11:13,205
of where power lay.

248
00:11:13,205 --> 00:11:15,274
You can take in
 the fish and meat tonight.

249
00:11:15,274 --> 00:11:17,476
James can follow
 with the sauce.

250
00:11:20,012 --> 00:11:21,847
But I should be
 the first footman.

251
00:11:21,847 --> 00:11:23,015
Of course you should.

252
00:11:23,015 --> 00:11:25,217
First footmen
 always take the meat.

253
00:11:25,217 --> 00:11:27,253
Look at him,
 he can't even balance it.

254
00:11:27,253 --> 00:11:29,221
SPELEERS:
 And the second footman
 will bring out the second,

255
00:11:29,221 --> 00:11:31,123
the sort of the side dish
 or the veg.

256
00:11:31,123 --> 00:11:32,725
It's a flipping insult--
 just because he's ten foot tall!

257
00:11:32,725 --> 00:11:34,260
SPELEERS:
 And some footmen got paid more
 for being taller.

258
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:36,696
So can you imagine Alfred and I,
 for example,

259
00:11:36,696 --> 00:11:38,664
I mean, he's six foot four,
 Matt Milne,

260
00:11:38,664 --> 00:11:39,832
and I'm five foot nine.

261
00:11:39,832 --> 00:11:41,100
So I mean, he'd have got paid
 loads of money

262
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:42,401
and I'd have been broke,
 basically.

263
00:11:44,136 --> 00:11:46,105
There's a lot of choreography

264
00:11:46,105 --> 00:11:48,474
that goes into operating
 as a footman.

265
00:11:48,474 --> 00:11:49,742
Do you want me to show you?

266
00:11:49,742 --> 00:11:51,410
The thing is, I need about
 four people to help me out,

267
00:11:51,410 --> 00:11:52,545
that's the problem.

268
00:11:52,545 --> 00:11:55,214
Just imagine
 if this was a long table.

269
00:11:55,214 --> 00:11:59,251
So we'd have Carson
 standing directly here.

270
00:11:59,251 --> 00:12:01,320
The other side,
 there's a working,

271
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:03,322
like I say, there's a fireplace
 working very well.

272
00:12:03,322 --> 00:12:05,257
You'd have me,
 so his first footman,

273
00:12:05,257 --> 00:12:06,992
like, ready to go,
 basically.

274
00:12:06,992 --> 00:12:10,296
I'm waiting for Carson's nod
 to go and serve food.

275
00:12:12,865 --> 00:12:16,135
So the first person I'd serve
 would be the Dowager.

276
00:12:16,135 --> 00:12:17,903
Don't put them like that.

277
00:12:17,903 --> 00:12:19,972
They've got to be able
 to get hold of them.

278
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:22,875
SPELEERS:
 This is Maggie Smith,

279
00:12:22,875 --> 00:12:24,543
this is
 an Oscar-winner here, okay.

280
00:12:24,543 --> 00:12:26,178
I'm very nervous every time

281
00:12:26,178 --> 00:12:28,280
I have to sort of come around
 the back of her to serve.

282
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:29,615
It's quite a nerve-wracking
 experience.

283
00:12:29,615 --> 00:12:31,050
Oh, lovely, what a treat.

284
00:12:31,050 --> 00:12:32,051
(clanging)

285
00:12:32,051 --> 00:12:33,552
Oh!

286
00:12:33,552 --> 00:12:35,488
But sometimes, I'm having
 to climb over dollies like this

287
00:12:35,488 --> 00:12:36,889
with a tray of, like, meat

288
00:12:36,889 --> 00:12:38,591
and come out
 and serve round like that,

289
00:12:38,591 --> 00:12:40,192
and still look poised
 and come in,

290
00:12:40,192 --> 00:12:41,427
and I lean in like that,
 as such.

291
00:12:41,427 --> 00:12:43,195
Mama, talk to her.

292
00:12:43,195 --> 00:12:44,396
Talk to all of them.

293
00:12:44,396 --> 00:12:45,631
Say something sensible.

294
00:12:45,631 --> 00:12:47,867
SPELEERS:
 And you've got to wait,
 and you cannot move.

295
00:12:47,867 --> 00:12:51,570
You go back, you wait,
 you move off.

296
00:12:51,570 --> 00:12:53,672
Now as I come in
 to my third person,

297
00:12:53,672 --> 00:12:55,674
Mr. Molesley,
 or the second footman,

298
00:12:55,674 --> 00:12:57,643
whoever that may be
 or is at the time,

299
00:12:57,643 --> 00:13:00,513
will come in and start
 on his main, the second dish.

300
00:13:00,513 --> 00:13:03,349
So peripherally, I can see him
 and we lean in together.

301
00:13:04,750 --> 00:13:06,752
DOYLE:
 They kind of have to be
 invisible.

302
00:13:06,752 --> 00:13:10,990
Nothing interferes with
 the sociability of the table.

303
00:13:13,993 --> 00:13:19,632
ELIZABETH McGOVERN:
 No one ever takes a bite
 before Cora does, the hostess.

304
00:13:19,632 --> 00:13:22,902
She will decide which direction
 the conversation is going.

305
00:13:24,370 --> 00:13:26,639
Are we ever going
 to be allowed to turn?

306
00:13:26,639 --> 00:13:29,708
HUGH BONNEVILLE:
 Turning in dinner,
 meaning that the conversation

307
00:13:29,708 --> 00:13:31,410
will start
 in a particular direction

308
00:13:31,410 --> 00:13:33,646
as set by the hostess, Cora.

309
00:13:33,646 --> 00:13:35,881
And so everyone
 will speak that way.

310
00:13:35,881 --> 00:13:38,417
And then after, you know,
 when she turns...

311
00:13:38,417 --> 00:13:39,852
It's time to turn.

312
00:13:39,852 --> 00:13:41,620
BONNEVILLE:
 Everyone turns and speaks
 to the person on that side,

313
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:43,255
so everyone's getting
 their fair share of chat.

314
00:13:43,255 --> 00:13:46,659
BRUCE:
 The apparently effortless ritual
 of the dining room

315
00:13:46,659 --> 00:13:49,461
disguised its true purpose.

316
00:13:49,461 --> 00:13:54,500
It was a place to influence,
 a place to exercise power,

317
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:56,468
a place to find a husband.

318
00:13:56,468 --> 00:13:57,736
MARY:
 How many times
 am I to be ordered

319
00:13:57,736 --> 00:13:59,872
to marry the man
 sitting next to me at dinner?

320
00:13:59,872 --> 00:14:01,941
As many times as it takes.

321
00:14:08,714 --> 00:14:12,818
Marriage among the ruling
 classes of Great Britain

322
00:14:12,818 --> 00:14:15,287
was seldom about love,

323
00:14:15,287 --> 00:14:18,991
but it was always
 about power and land.

324
00:14:18,991 --> 00:14:23,329
Land must, above all,
 be kept in one piece

325
00:14:23,329 --> 00:14:26,665
in order to give a man
 the income to live by

326
00:14:26,665 --> 00:14:30,169
and the right by which
 he could rule the country.

327
00:14:30,169 --> 00:14:32,171
I believe there is an answer

328
00:14:32,171 --> 00:14:36,242
which would secure your future
 and give you a position.

329
00:14:36,242 --> 00:14:38,544
You can't be serious.

330
00:14:38,544 --> 00:14:40,045
Just think about it.

331
00:14:40,045 --> 00:14:41,380
I don't have
 to think about it.

332
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:45,117
Marry a man who can barely hold
 his knife like a gentleman?

333
00:14:45,117 --> 00:14:48,020
Oh, you exaggerate.

334
00:14:50,556 --> 00:14:51,957
BRUCE:
 Women had absolutely

335
00:14:51,957 --> 00:14:55,060
no position or power
 until they were married,

336
00:14:55,060 --> 00:14:57,863
so they had to find a man
 who did.

337
00:14:57,863 --> 00:14:59,131
Sir Anthony Strallan.

338
00:14:59,131 --> 00:15:00,399
Sir Anthony!

339
00:15:00,399 --> 00:15:01,834
Don't worry, Lady Grantham,
 I haven't got the date wrong.

340
00:15:01,834 --> 00:15:03,736
LAURA CARMICHAEL:
 Women had a difficult time.

341
00:15:03,736 --> 00:15:07,673
They didn't stand to gain
 what sons would have done.

342
00:15:07,673 --> 00:15:10,476
They had to find their fortune
 in a good match,

343
00:15:10,476 --> 00:15:11,644
in a good marriage.

344
00:15:11,644 --> 00:15:13,212
The thing is,
 I've got two tickets

345
00:15:13,212 --> 00:15:14,647
for a concert in York

346
00:15:14,647 --> 00:15:15,915
next Friday.

347
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:17,349
How nice.

348
00:15:17,349 --> 00:15:19,518
Although I can't...

349
00:15:19,518 --> 00:15:22,655
No, I was hoping that Lady Edith
 might like to accompany me.

350
00:15:22,655 --> 00:15:24,089
But I'd love to.

351
00:15:24,089 --> 00:15:24,823
Excellent.

352
00:15:25,824 --> 00:15:28,127
LAURA CARMICHAEL:
 It was very important.

353
00:15:28,127 --> 00:15:30,029
It was what women
 were focused on

354
00:15:30,029 --> 00:15:32,898
because it was the way
 to leave home

355
00:15:32,898 --> 00:15:35,134
and leave your parents behind
 at last

356
00:15:35,134 --> 00:15:37,670
and set up your own home.

357
00:15:37,670 --> 00:15:40,940
BRUCE:
 A young lady could not marry

358
00:15:40,940 --> 00:15:44,376
until she had been presented
 at court.

359
00:15:44,376 --> 00:15:47,579
It was the start of a busy year
 known as her "coming out."

360
00:15:47,579 --> 00:15:49,581
JAMES:
 I suppose "coming out"

361
00:15:49,581 --> 00:15:54,753
was the most extreme example
 of these rules and etiquette.

362
00:15:54,753 --> 00:16:00,459
You had a train over your hair,
 and the length of the train

363
00:16:00,459 --> 00:16:04,129
was absolutely,
 to a centimeter, controlled.

364
00:16:04,129 --> 00:16:06,165
BRUCE:
 From shoulder to floor,

365
00:16:06,165 --> 00:16:08,934
trains measured no less
 than three yards.

366
00:16:08,934 --> 00:16:11,270
JAMES:
 You just dressed up in white

367
00:16:11,270 --> 00:16:13,973
with your feathers,
 and it's all really specific,

368
00:16:13,973 --> 00:16:15,607
how many feathers you have
 in your hair.

369
00:16:15,607 --> 00:16:19,478
BRUCE:
 White dresses signified purity.

370
00:16:19,478 --> 00:16:22,548
The number of feathers said
 who was who.

371
00:16:22,548 --> 00:16:27,219
Unmarried young ladies wore two,
 married chaperones wore three.

372
00:16:27,219 --> 00:16:28,754
MAN:
 The Countess of Grantham,

373
00:16:28,754 --> 00:16:32,758
presenting the
 Lady Rose MacClare.

374
00:16:32,758 --> 00:16:35,728
BRUCE:
 A young lady's presentation
 to the king and queen

375
00:16:35,728 --> 00:16:38,530
was her entry into society.

376
00:16:38,530 --> 00:16:40,466
It signaled she had
 the monarch's blessing

377
00:16:40,466 --> 00:16:44,003
as a suitable wife.

378
00:16:48,507 --> 00:16:49,708
CREW:
 Sound, speed.

379
00:16:53,645 --> 00:16:55,147
And action.

380
00:16:55,147 --> 00:16:56,949
That frock
 really is yummy.

381
00:16:56,949 --> 00:16:58,017
Thank you.

382
00:16:58,017 --> 00:17:00,386
BRUCE:
 Once she'd been presented
 at court,

383
00:17:00,386 --> 00:17:03,122
a young woman could attend
 what was known as "the season,"

384
00:17:03,122 --> 00:17:08,027
a round of parties where
 she would meet eligible men.

385
00:17:08,027 --> 00:17:09,528
How come I'm not
 in my lieutenant's gear?

386
00:17:09,528 --> 00:17:11,497
Because they're not...

387
00:17:11,497 --> 00:17:12,898
There is no evening dress

388
00:17:12,898 --> 00:17:16,735
for members of the lieutenancy
 at this period.

389
00:17:16,735 --> 00:17:18,370
Alastair is very useful

390
00:17:18,370 --> 00:17:20,773
in providing context
 and providing detail,

391
00:17:20,773 --> 00:17:23,575
particularly around
 the interaction between people.

392
00:17:23,575 --> 00:17:26,178
I hope you got
 everything done.

393
00:17:26,178 --> 00:17:28,447
We wanted to clink our glasses
 and say "cheers."

394
00:17:28,447 --> 00:17:32,351
Well, women wouldn't have
 clinked their glasses.

395
00:17:32,351 --> 00:17:35,087
Cut and reset, please.

396
00:17:35,087 --> 00:17:37,389
And they also wouldn't
 have said "cheers."

397
00:17:37,389 --> 00:17:39,591
So he sort of swooped in.

398
00:17:39,591 --> 00:17:40,692
Can we talk
 about that glass?

399
00:17:40,692 --> 00:17:41,693
About saying "cheers"?

400
00:17:41,693 --> 00:17:42,561
Yes, exactly right.

401
00:17:42,561 --> 00:17:44,129
I'll just go,
 "Your good health."

402
00:17:44,129 --> 00:17:45,531
You want to say,
 "Your good health."

403
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:46,532
CREW:
 Quiet, please.

404
00:17:46,532 --> 00:17:48,167
And turnover.

405
00:17:48,167 --> 00:17:50,402
Turning, turning, action!

406
00:17:50,402 --> 00:17:51,937
CULLEN:
 I love Alastair.

407
00:17:51,937 --> 00:17:54,006
I mean, he literally
 taught me everything--

408
00:17:54,006 --> 00:17:57,743
how to tie my shoelaces,
 how to gesture,

409
00:17:57,743 --> 00:18:00,145
how to stand, ride a horse.

410
00:18:00,145 --> 00:18:04,516
Um, he taught me
 about the history of my family.

411
00:18:04,516 --> 00:18:08,787
He's got this thing he calls
 the posh bible,

412
00:18:08,787 --> 00:18:11,123
which is this enormous book,

413
00:18:11,123 --> 00:18:14,726
which I was...
 I couldn't believe existed.

414
00:18:14,726 --> 00:18:16,061
So he's in it.

415
00:18:16,061 --> 00:18:17,596
Alastair can trace his line

416
00:18:17,596 --> 00:18:18,931
all the way back
 to Robert the Bruce,

417
00:18:18,931 --> 00:18:22,768
you know, and further,
 and they're all in this book.

418
00:18:22,768 --> 00:18:25,871
BRUCE:
 That thunderous book
 is Burke's Peerage,

419
00:18:25,871 --> 00:18:28,273
a list of every titled
 aristocrat

420
00:18:28,273 --> 00:18:29,441
in the United Kingdom.

421
00:18:29,441 --> 00:18:33,178
A combination of background
 check and bloodline,

422
00:18:33,178 --> 00:18:35,981
an essential tool
 for making a match.

423
00:18:35,981 --> 00:18:37,449
CULLEN:
 It's got nothing...

424
00:18:37,449 --> 00:18:38,951
It's got absolutely nothing
 to do with money.

425
00:18:38,951 --> 00:18:41,220
This is what
 the posh bible is about.

426
00:18:41,220 --> 00:18:42,988
It's to show that it's got
 nothing to do with money;

427
00:18:42,988 --> 00:18:45,357
it's about your lineage
 and your line

428
00:18:45,357 --> 00:18:47,860
and about where you come from.

429
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:51,063
BRUCE:
 Parents always used
 Burke's Peerage

430
00:18:51,063 --> 00:18:55,234
to check the family title
 and status of potential suitors.

431
00:18:55,234 --> 00:18:58,971
Their aim: a marriage
 that enhanced the family.

432
00:18:58,971 --> 00:19:00,539
CULLEN:
 Lord Gillingham is able

433
00:19:00,539 --> 00:19:03,976
to trace his lineage back
 hundreds of years.

434
00:19:03,976 --> 00:19:06,812
And so he has to marry
 into certain families,

435
00:19:06,812 --> 00:19:08,714
and there are certain names
 and titles

436
00:19:08,714 --> 00:19:11,350
that you have to match up to.

437
00:19:11,350 --> 00:19:14,753
And it's like
 a game of chess, politics.

438
00:19:14,753 --> 00:19:16,488
I don't need
 to explain to you

439
00:19:16,488 --> 00:19:17,990
how the system
 we're trapped in works.

440
00:19:17,990 --> 00:19:21,894
Please don't rush
 into anything.

441
00:19:21,894 --> 00:19:24,263
I won't make a fool
 of Mabel.

442
00:19:24,263 --> 00:19:25,998
It wouldn't be fair.

443
00:19:25,998 --> 00:19:29,134
CULLEN:
 The system that
 they're trapped in,

444
00:19:29,134 --> 00:19:31,170
it's a set of rules, you know.

445
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:33,071
I mean, this is the thing
 that shocked me most

446
00:19:33,071 --> 00:19:35,107
about playing Lord Gillingham

447
00:19:35,107 --> 00:19:38,710
was how repressed
 and how stifled everything is.

448
00:19:38,710 --> 00:19:42,314
Like how little emotion
 you're allowed to show.

449
00:19:42,314 --> 00:19:44,416
You're not allowed to laugh,
 you're not allowed to love,

450
00:19:44,416 --> 00:19:45,784
you're not allowed to...

451
00:19:45,784 --> 00:19:47,553
You're certainly
 not allowed to cry.

452
00:19:47,553 --> 00:19:51,590
Um, and you have to marry

453
00:19:51,590 --> 00:19:55,360
the people that your family
 ask you to marry.

454
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:59,765
And so when, you know,
 Gillingham says to Mary,

455
00:19:59,765 --> 00:20:03,001
"We both understand the system
 that we're trapped in,"

456
00:20:03,001 --> 00:20:06,338
I think, for the first time
 in his life,

457
00:20:06,338 --> 00:20:09,208
he's telling somebody
 that he loves them.

458
00:20:18,383 --> 00:20:20,385
CREW:
 Quiet, please!

459
00:20:20,385 --> 00:20:23,121
Locking up outside.

460
00:20:23,121 --> 00:20:24,890
How and who to marry

461
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:27,125
was fraught with complications
 upstairs.

462
00:20:27,125 --> 00:20:30,796
Downstairs, it was simple:
 you just didn't.

463
00:20:30,796 --> 00:20:32,965
VIOLET:
 I was right about my maid.

464
00:20:32,965 --> 00:20:36,068
She's leaving
 to get married.

465
00:20:36,068 --> 00:20:38,070
How could she be
 so selfish?

466
00:20:38,070 --> 00:20:41,506
Courtship is not permitted
 amongst servants,

467
00:20:41,506 --> 00:20:43,008
amongst the staff.

468
00:20:43,008 --> 00:20:44,543
I don't know what...

469
00:20:44,543 --> 00:20:45,944
Shh!

470
00:20:45,944 --> 00:20:47,446
COYLE:
 It was frowned upon,

471
00:20:47,446 --> 00:20:51,383
so it's a very gentle,
 drawn-out process.

472
00:20:58,924 --> 00:21:00,058
Which I loved.

473
00:21:00,058 --> 00:21:02,361
I love that process,
 we both did,

474
00:21:02,361 --> 00:21:06,265
so it was a very tender,
 very delicate, incremental path

475
00:21:06,265 --> 00:21:08,133
towards togetherness.

476
00:21:09,735 --> 00:21:11,770
That's enough of that,
 Mr. Bates.

477
00:21:11,770 --> 00:21:13,272
We've work to do.

478
00:21:16,174 --> 00:21:17,976
It did happen that servants
 got married,

479
00:21:17,976 --> 00:21:21,013
but actually in reality, the
 woman would have left service.

480
00:21:21,013 --> 00:21:24,116
BRUCE:
 Marriage challenged your loyalty
 to the family.

481
00:21:24,116 --> 00:21:27,419
Service had to be your life.

482
00:21:27,419 --> 00:21:30,789
And there was
 a practical reason too:

483
00:21:30,789 --> 00:21:35,193
men and women had separate
 sleeping quarters in the house.

484
00:21:35,193 --> 00:21:37,796
There was nowhere
 for married couples to live.

485
00:21:37,796 --> 00:21:40,432
So they're in quite a privileged
 position really, Anna and Bates.

486
00:21:40,432 --> 00:21:44,269
SOPHIE McSHERA:
 Courting doesn't seem
 to be really done downstairs

487
00:21:44,269 --> 00:21:45,771
for Daisy especially,

488
00:21:45,771 --> 00:21:49,374
because if she met him,
 she'd have to go off with him,

489
00:21:49,374 --> 00:21:51,543
so it would be rubbish for me,
 I'd have to leave.

490
00:21:51,543 --> 00:21:53,812
And there's also no time
 to do much courting.

491
00:21:53,812 --> 00:21:55,947
Bring the sauce boats for
 Alfred, I'm doing the soufflés.

492
00:21:55,947 --> 00:21:56,682
As soon as I've...

493
00:21:56,682 --> 00:21:57,916
Will you just do it!

494
00:21:58,917 --> 00:22:00,052
McSHERA:
 You couldn't, you know,

495
00:22:00,052 --> 00:22:01,953
court, go on a date,

496
00:22:01,953 --> 00:22:04,456
and snog someone
 or something like that.

497
00:22:04,456 --> 00:22:08,627
I think that's why Daisy's
 having such bad luck with men,

498
00:22:08,627 --> 00:22:10,529
because she's basically got
 the people around her

499
00:22:10,529 --> 00:22:12,197
and that's it,
 she never gets out.

500
00:22:12,197 --> 00:22:13,999
Taxi!

501
00:22:13,999 --> 00:22:16,968
BRUCE:
 Aristocratic daughters
 could go out.

502
00:22:16,968 --> 00:22:20,305
But to meet a man alone
 without a chaperone

503
00:22:20,305 --> 00:22:23,275
was to risk their
 good name forever.

504
00:22:23,275 --> 00:22:28,180
(lively jazz playing)

505
00:22:28,180 --> 00:22:30,382
JAMES:
 I love that she's a party girl

506
00:22:30,382 --> 00:22:33,418
at a time when you had
 no independence.

507
00:22:33,418 --> 00:22:35,987
Actually, she's being
 incredibly brave

