525
00:23:25,337 --> 00:23:28,206
to have a baby
 out of wedlock.

526
00:23:30,175 --> 00:23:31,643
Alastair sort of said to me,

527
00:23:31,643 --> 00:23:34,079
he said, "All of this,"
 pointing to the castle,

528
00:23:34,079 --> 00:23:38,884
"Is based on the idea that you
 will behave properly

529
00:23:38,884 --> 00:23:42,020
"and you will be sort of someone
 for people to look up to.

530
00:23:42,020 --> 00:23:43,822
"That's what is expected.

531
00:23:43,822 --> 00:23:48,293
"And if you don't
 and, you know, you are shamed,

532
00:23:48,293 --> 00:23:53,632
"then that really will destroy
 your reputation in the county.

533
00:23:53,632 --> 00:23:55,867
You know,
 it'll go around London."

534
00:23:55,867 --> 00:23:59,137
So in this series, there really
 is a danger for Edith.

535
00:23:59,137 --> 00:24:03,074
Isn't it time one of you
 told me the truth?

536
00:24:03,074 --> 00:24:05,343
If I told you the truth, Granny,

537
00:24:05,343 --> 00:24:06,978
you'd never speak
 to me again.

538
00:24:06,978 --> 00:24:09,381
Then you have
 told me the truth.

539
00:24:16,321 --> 00:24:17,556
You'll share
 the ownership.

540
00:24:17,556 --> 00:24:19,090
It will be your house,
 your estate as much as mine.

541
00:24:19,090 --> 00:24:20,292
We will be
 joint masters.

542
00:24:20,292 --> 00:24:21,159
But...

543
00:24:29,067 --> 00:24:30,669
BRUCE:
 I love the glorious formality

544
00:24:30,669 --> 00:24:32,804
of Edwardian life.

545
00:24:32,804 --> 00:24:35,674
The rules they had to behave by
 were a mixture

546
00:24:35,674 --> 00:24:39,511
of straightforward practicality
 and fear.

547
00:24:39,511 --> 00:24:42,147
BONNEVILLE:
 We shot a version

548
00:24:42,147 --> 00:24:44,049
where our instincts were
 entirely to hug,

549
00:24:44,049 --> 00:24:45,383
as you would these days.

550
00:24:45,383 --> 00:24:46,651
And of course
 Alastair, you know,

551
00:24:46,651 --> 00:24:48,220
threw his toys out of the pram
 and said, "Don't be ridiculous.

552
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:51,389
You might just shake his hand--
 just-- to express an emotion."

553
00:24:51,389 --> 00:24:53,391
(laughing)

554
00:24:53,391 --> 00:24:57,028
You are my darling daughter
 and I love you,

555
00:24:57,028 --> 00:25:00,098
hard as it is for an Englishman
 to say the words.

556
00:25:00,098 --> 00:25:04,736
BRUCE:
 The British hadn't always
 maintained a stiff upper lip.

557
00:25:04,736 --> 00:25:07,072
Only a century earlier,

558
00:25:07,072 --> 00:25:08,974
the passions
 of the French Revolution

559
00:25:08,974 --> 00:25:10,842
so terrified
 the British aristocracy

560
00:25:10,842 --> 00:25:15,514
that a cool reserve became the
 definition of good character.

561
00:25:15,514 --> 00:25:19,684
There was a very real fear
 that without formality,

562
00:25:19,684 --> 00:25:22,420
the authority
 of the aristocracy would slip

563
00:25:22,420 --> 00:25:24,923
and society would fall apart.

564
00:25:24,923 --> 00:25:27,526
So the aristocracy
 cultivated manners

565
00:25:27,526 --> 00:25:29,728
that protected their position.

566
00:25:29,728 --> 00:25:31,062
You don't understand.

567
00:25:31,062 --> 00:25:32,464
I shall be Countess
 of Grantham one day,

568
00:25:32,464 --> 00:25:33,932
and in my book,

569
00:25:33,932 --> 00:25:37,769
the Countess of Grantham
 lives at Downton Abbey.

570
00:25:37,769 --> 00:25:39,271
Being who they are

571
00:25:39,271 --> 00:25:41,006
and the position
 that they're in,

572
00:25:41,006 --> 00:25:44,009
they are very outspoken people.

573
00:25:44,009 --> 00:25:46,278
You know, Mary is someone
 that if she has an opinion,

574
00:25:46,278 --> 00:25:48,280
she'll say it,
 and she says it with conviction

575
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:50,215
and she doesn't apologize
 for it.

576
00:25:50,215 --> 00:25:52,117
The tax people
 have had a cancellation,

577
00:25:52,117 --> 00:25:53,952
so they can see us
 on Wednesday at noon.

578
00:25:53,952 --> 00:25:56,488
But I think we should
 go up tomorrow.

579
00:25:56,488 --> 00:25:58,990
I wouldn't like to risk
 being late.

580
00:25:58,990 --> 00:26:00,225
It's confidence, actually.

581
00:26:00,225 --> 00:26:01,893
It's having the confidence

582
00:26:01,893 --> 00:26:05,463
to follow through
 everything you say

583
00:26:05,463 --> 00:26:09,034
and every thought you have
 with conviction.

584
00:26:09,034 --> 00:26:11,269
The world moves on
 and we must move with it.

585
00:26:11,269 --> 00:26:12,604
So you keep telling me.

586
00:26:12,604 --> 00:26:14,272
They spoke very loudly

587
00:26:14,272 --> 00:26:16,308
because, you know, they had
 a right to be heard

588
00:26:16,308 --> 00:26:18,076
and they weren't afraid
 of their opinion,

589
00:26:18,076 --> 00:26:20,245
and everyone listened
 when they spoke

590
00:26:20,245 --> 00:26:23,114
because they're the aristocracy,
 and that's that!

591
00:26:23,114 --> 00:26:26,451
And also, these vast,
 big rooms they spoke in.

592
00:26:26,451 --> 00:26:27,419
When's Cousin Robert
 coming back?

593
00:26:27,419 --> 00:26:29,154
I don't know,
 I wish I did.

594
00:26:29,154 --> 00:26:31,856
We've had no word
 from him in days.

595
00:26:31,856 --> 00:26:33,058
CULLEN:
 The oddest thing is that

596
00:26:33,058 --> 00:26:35,794
you have to be incredibly rude
 to be an Edwardian.

597
00:26:35,794 --> 00:26:38,563
And posh, you have to be
 very rude to be posh.

598
00:26:38,563 --> 00:26:40,231
We don't say thank you
 for things.

599
00:26:40,231 --> 00:26:41,833
When you're given a cup of tea,

600
00:26:41,833 --> 00:26:45,303
you wouldn't acknowledge the
 person who's giving it to you.

601
00:26:45,303 --> 00:26:48,340
So you pretend that
 they're not there.

602
00:26:48,340 --> 00:26:52,377
All my instincts inside scream
 that this is so rude,

603
00:26:52,377 --> 00:26:54,045
this is so rude
 to be ignoring the person

604
00:26:54,045 --> 00:26:55,347
who's helping you out,
 doing something.

605
00:26:55,347 --> 00:26:57,182
But it's just the way it was.

606
00:26:58,850 --> 00:27:04,389
BRUCE:
 The way it was was simply
 practical to the Edwardians.

607
00:27:04,389 --> 00:27:06,257
I don't want you
 to stand in line.

608
00:27:06,257 --> 00:27:07,692
I want you to rush
 to that door.

609
00:27:07,692 --> 00:27:12,097
To be served 30 times a day
 and to say thank you every time

610
00:27:12,097 --> 00:27:13,765
would have been maddening.

611
00:27:13,765 --> 00:27:17,435
So the rule was
 silent appreciation.

612
00:27:17,435 --> 00:27:19,871
CARMICHAEL:
 The servants that
 pride themselves

613
00:27:19,871 --> 00:27:21,406
on being the best servants

614
00:27:21,406 --> 00:27:23,541
are practically, you know,
 invisible in those moments.

615
00:27:23,541 --> 00:27:25,944
My husband is dead.

616
00:27:25,944 --> 00:27:28,380
Can't you understand
 what that means?

617
00:27:28,380 --> 00:27:30,215
After all he suffered
 in the war,

618
00:27:30,215 --> 00:27:32,017
he's killed
 in a stupid car crash.

619
00:27:32,017 --> 00:27:33,752
SPELEERS:
 We see everything,
 we hear everything,

620
00:27:33,752 --> 00:27:35,520
but we've got to behave
 like we don't,

621
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:37,922
so almost like a blank face.

622
00:27:37,922 --> 00:27:39,958
But there's a lot of information
 that can be taken in obviously

623
00:27:39,958 --> 00:27:42,160
because it's impossible
 not to take it in.

624
00:27:42,160 --> 00:27:44,729
Well, they do, they just talk
 in front of the servants

625
00:27:44,729 --> 00:27:47,098
because they assume that
 it will go over their heads.

626
00:27:47,098 --> 00:27:48,333
Well, of course it doesn't.

627
00:27:48,333 --> 00:27:50,268
TOM:
 He's asked me
 to go in with him.

628
00:27:50,268 --> 00:27:51,970
A brother
 who's coming to stay?

629
00:27:51,970 --> 00:27:54,072
Yes-- Kieran.

630
00:27:54,072 --> 00:27:56,041
Also, that's the entertainment
 for the people downstairs.

631
00:27:56,041 --> 00:27:57,809
You know, for everyone
 downstairs, for the staff,

632
00:27:57,809 --> 00:27:59,144
that's their soap opera.

633
00:28:00,278 --> 00:28:02,247
BRUCE:
 However dramatic
 the entertainment,

634
00:28:02,247 --> 00:28:05,450
the servants were trusted
 to be utterly discreet.

635
00:28:05,450 --> 00:28:08,253
Is there some crisis
 of which I am unaware?

636
00:28:08,253 --> 00:28:09,454
No, Mr. Carson.

637
00:28:09,454 --> 00:28:13,058
The first rule of being a
 servant upstairs is discretion.

638
00:28:13,058 --> 00:28:15,860
We do not discuss the business
 of this house with strangers.

639
00:28:15,860 --> 00:28:19,030
They may gossip
 between themselves downstairs,

640
00:28:19,030 --> 00:28:20,865
but it certainly
 wouldn't be something

641
00:28:20,865 --> 00:28:23,368
they'd want
 to leave the house

642
00:28:23,368 --> 00:28:25,370
because they wouldn't want
 to be working for the house

643
00:28:25,370 --> 00:28:26,805
that was center of the gossip.

644
00:28:26,805 --> 00:28:28,440
You know, they want to be seen

645
00:28:28,440 --> 00:28:30,742
as working for one of the best
 houses in the country,

646
00:28:30,742 --> 00:28:32,410
you know, because that's
 their status symbol.

647
00:28:32,410 --> 00:28:35,180
BRUCE:
 The Edwardians were terrified
 of scandal

648
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:38,817
because the newspapers
 had just discovered

649
00:28:38,817 --> 00:28:41,352
how marketable
 gossip could be.

650
00:28:41,352 --> 00:28:44,656
For the first time,
 tittle-tattle wasn't just local;

651
00:28:44,656 --> 00:28:47,892
the whole country
 could suddenly know your shame.

652
00:28:47,892 --> 00:28:49,360
CORA:
 What happened?

653
00:28:49,360 --> 00:28:51,096
I don't know!

654
00:28:51,096 --> 00:28:53,498
Yes, somebody died in Lady
 Mary's bed, goodness gracious.

655
00:28:53,498 --> 00:28:55,834
He was alive
 and suddenly he cried out

656
00:28:55,834 --> 00:28:57,202
and then he was dead.

657
00:28:57,202 --> 00:28:59,904
(whispering):
 But why was he here at all?

658
00:28:59,904 --> 00:29:02,173
FROGGATT:
 And you know,
 if anyone found out

659
00:29:02,173 --> 00:29:06,778
about this incident,
 Lady Mary would really be

660
00:29:06,778 --> 00:29:09,414
a pariah, you know,
 from society.

661
00:29:09,414 --> 00:29:11,683
It would be a terrible thing.

662
00:29:11,683 --> 00:29:13,017
I'll be ruined, Mama.

663
00:29:13,017 --> 00:29:14,319
Ruined and notorious.

664
00:29:14,319 --> 00:29:16,521
A laughing stock,
 a social pariah.

665
00:29:16,521 --> 00:29:19,224
Is that what you want
 for your eldest daughter?

666
00:29:19,224 --> 00:29:21,960
Is it what you want
 for the family?

667
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:23,294
So for the good of the house

668
00:29:23,294 --> 00:29:25,029
and for the good
 of Lady Mary's reputation,

669
00:29:25,029 --> 00:29:26,664
you know, it's Anna's job

670
00:29:26,664 --> 00:29:29,134
to go above and beyond
 the call of duty

671
00:29:29,134 --> 00:29:32,771
and help move a dead body
 from time to time.

672
00:29:32,771 --> 00:29:33,872
(groans)

673
00:29:33,872 --> 00:29:35,140
Mama!
 Sorry!

674
00:29:37,275 --> 00:29:40,011
McGOVERN:
 Cora has no other choice
 in the Pamuk situation.

675
00:29:40,011 --> 00:29:41,980
I mean, she's very well aware

676
00:29:41,980 --> 00:29:45,583
that it does mean
 absolute social death for Mary

677
00:29:45,583 --> 00:29:47,719
on all levels
 at a very young age.

678
00:29:47,719 --> 00:29:50,722
And, um, she's very much aware

679
00:29:50,722 --> 00:29:53,758
of what a terrible blow
 that would be for her.

680
00:29:53,758 --> 00:29:54,793
Anna.

681
00:29:56,694 --> 00:29:58,329
I will not insult you

682
00:29:58,329 --> 00:30:02,400
by asking that you also conceal
 Lady Mary's shame.

683
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:04,803
Let us go.

684
00:30:11,543 --> 00:30:14,813
If the first rule of being
 a good servant is discretion,

685
00:30:14,813 --> 00:30:18,383
then the second is never
 to burden your master

686
00:30:18,383 --> 00:30:20,051
with your own problems.

687
00:30:22,187 --> 00:30:24,022
I'm John Bates, the new valet.

688
00:30:24,022 --> 00:30:25,356
The new valet?

689
00:30:25,356 --> 00:30:26,357
That's right.

690
00:30:26,357 --> 00:30:27,592
COYLE:
 He arrives at the house

691
00:30:27,592 --> 00:30:28,760
and he's not welcome at all,

692
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:30,028
and he's not
 up to the task.

693
00:30:32,530 --> 00:30:35,300
So Lord Grantham says
 basically,

694
00:30:35,300 --> 00:30:37,836
"This isn't working,
 I'm gonna have to let you go."

695
00:30:37,836 --> 00:30:39,637
The thing is, Bates,

696
00:30:39,637 --> 00:30:43,007
I said I'd give you a trial
 and I have.

697
00:30:43,007 --> 00:30:44,075
COYLE:
 Losing the job would mean,

698
00:30:44,075 --> 00:30:46,477
for a man of his age
 and this disability,

699
00:30:46,477 --> 00:30:48,580
it would be straight
 to the workhouse.

700
00:30:48,580 --> 00:30:49,848
It would be over.

701
00:30:49,848 --> 00:30:51,516
You do see that Carson
 can't be expected

702
00:30:51,516 --> 00:30:54,052
to compromise the efficiency
 of his staff.

703
00:30:54,052 --> 00:30:56,621
I do, m'lord,
 of course I do.

704
00:30:56,621 --> 00:30:59,757
Bates wants to implore,
 as anybody would.

705
00:30:59,757 --> 00:31:02,493
He'd want to implore this man,
 "Please, I'll do anything.

706
00:31:02,493 --> 00:31:04,295
"What can I do?

707
00:31:04,295 --> 00:31:05,797
"I'll try this.

708
00:31:05,797 --> 00:31:07,665
Well, how can we work this out?"

709
00:31:07,665 --> 00:31:10,702
But he can't and he wouldn't.

710
00:31:10,702 --> 00:31:11,870
So he says...

711
00:31:11,870 --> 00:31:13,938
Might I make
 a suggestion that

712
00:31:13,938 --> 00:31:15,673
when an extra footman
 is required,

713
00:31:15,673 --> 00:31:17,075
the costs could come out
 of my wages.

714
00:31:17,075 --> 00:31:19,611
Absolutely not,
 I couldn't possibly allow that.

715
00:31:19,611 --> 00:31:22,013
Because I am very eager
 to stay, m'lord.

716
00:31:22,013 --> 00:31:24,949
Very eager indeed.

717
00:31:24,949 --> 00:31:29,053
It's very delicately put,
 but it's loaded with a pleading

718
00:31:29,053 --> 00:31:33,391
which the man doesn't express,
 nor would he.

719
00:31:33,391 --> 00:31:36,494
And once Lord Grantham says...

720
00:31:36,494 --> 00:31:39,964
I mean to help
 until you find something.

721
00:31:39,964 --> 00:31:42,667
There's a finality to that
 that both men know.

722
00:31:44,602 --> 00:31:45,970
Very good, m'lord.

723
00:31:45,970 --> 00:31:47,138
I'll go at once.

724
00:31:47,138 --> 00:31:48,740
There's no need to rush out
 into the night.

725
00:31:48,740 --> 00:31:50,241
Take the London train tomorrow.

726
00:31:50,241 --> 00:31:51,776
It leaves at 9:00.

727
00:31:51,776 --> 00:31:52,810
COYLE:
 And ostensibly,

728
00:31:52,810 --> 00:31:55,413
it's over for Bates.

729
00:31:55,413 --> 00:31:59,550
All in that tiny scene,
 so much that is unsaid is said.

730
00:32:00,885 --> 00:32:02,420
It's a bloody business,
 Bates,

731
00:32:02,420 --> 00:32:05,089
but I can't see
 any way around it.

732
00:32:05,089 --> 00:32:06,691
I quite understand, m'lord.

733
00:32:09,560 --> 00:32:12,530
COYLE:
 It would be unseemly, it would
 be crude to be emotional,

734
00:32:12,530 --> 00:32:15,767
and to impose that on somebody

735
00:32:15,767 --> 00:32:20,171
would put them
 in an awkward position.

736
00:32:22,540 --> 00:32:24,943
There are many records
 of servants

737
00:32:24,943 --> 00:32:28,346
giving emotional support
 to their masters and mistresses.

738
00:32:28,346 --> 00:32:30,982
But if the servants
 face troubles,

739
00:32:30,982 --> 00:32:32,951
they'd probably face them alone.

740
00:32:32,951 --> 00:32:36,187
What I want is
 to go back upstairs.

741
00:32:36,187 --> 00:32:37,722
You're not telling me
 that sad old cripple

742
00:32:37,722 --> 00:32:39,624
keeps you happy.

743
00:32:39,624 --> 00:32:44,195
If you must know, yes,
 he keeps me very happy.

744
00:32:44,195 --> 00:32:47,031
Now let me by, please.

745
00:32:47,031 --> 00:32:49,434
Perhaps you've forgotten
 what you're missing.

746
00:32:49,434 --> 00:32:53,071
FROGGATT:
 When I found out
 about Anna's rape storyline,

747
00:32:53,071 --> 00:32:55,073
I was, you know,
 trying to get my head around

748
00:32:55,073 --> 00:32:58,710
what that meant for a woman
 of her class in her time.

749
00:32:58,710 --> 00:33:00,311
Because, you know,
 when I read the scripts,

750
00:33:00,311 --> 00:33:01,913
I, like a lot of other people,

751
00:33:01,913 --> 00:33:04,382
really wanted Anna
 to tell someone,

752
00:33:04,382 --> 00:33:06,751
for this man to be brought
 to justice, you know?

753
00:33:06,751 --> 00:33:09,320
But Alastair said to me,

754
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:11,656
"You know, you have to remember
 for a working-class woman,

755
00:33:11,656 --> 00:33:13,358
all she had was her reputation."

756
00:33:13,358 --> 00:33:15,026
We must tell somebody.

757
00:33:15,026 --> 00:33:16,894
No, no, no, no.

758
00:33:16,894 --> 00:33:18,296
But you have
 to tell Mr. Bates.

759
00:33:18,296 --> 00:33:19,564
Him least of all!

760
00:33:19,564 --> 00:33:21,232
If he knew, he'd murder
 the man who's done it

761
00:33:21,232 --> 00:33:22,233
and then he'd be hanged.

762
00:33:22,233 --> 00:33:23,868
FROGGATT:
 In reality,

763
00:33:23,868 --> 00:33:25,970
if anybody would have found out
 what happened to her,

764
00:33:25,970 --> 00:33:29,207
then she would have, you know,
 brought disrepute on the house,

765
00:33:29,207 --> 00:33:31,175
she would have brought disrepute
 on herself,

766
00:33:31,175 --> 00:33:32,844
she probably would have
 lost her job.

767
00:33:32,844 --> 00:33:34,912
Nobody else must ever know.

768
00:33:34,912 --> 00:33:36,581
You promise me?

769
00:33:36,581 --> 00:33:38,850
FROGGATT:
 To us, that sounds totally
 barbaric, thank goodness.

770
00:33:38,850 --> 00:33:43,321
You know, it's remembering we're
 only 100 years on from that.

771
00:33:43,321 --> 00:33:46,624
And that was a real
 eye-opener for me.

772
00:33:46,624 --> 00:33:50,561
It's a strange relationship
 between servants and masters--

773
00:33:50,561 --> 00:33:54,265
very close,
 but bound by rules.

774
00:33:54,265 --> 00:33:56,134
(bell rings)

775
00:33:56,134 --> 00:33:58,036
There are certain rules
 within the house.

776
00:33:58,036 --> 00:33:59,837
There is the etiquette,
 there is the decorum,

777
00:33:59,837 --> 00:34:01,773
there is the upkeep,
 there are the rules.

778
00:34:01,773 --> 00:34:03,741
You would never ask
 a personal question.

779
00:34:03,741 --> 00:34:05,009
Good day, m'lord?

780
00:34:05,009 --> 00:34:06,344
Good until tonight.

781
00:34:06,344 --> 00:34:08,479
I took a walloping
 from Mr. Sampson.

782
00:34:08,479 --> 00:34:10,581
You would never prompt
 a conversation.

783
00:34:10,581 --> 00:34:12,250
I was a fool
 to play with someone

784
00:34:12,250 --> 00:34:14,318
who so obviously knew
 what he was doing.

785
00:34:14,318 --> 00:34:16,554
COYLE:
 You certainly wouldn't say
 anything judgmental

786
00:34:16,554 --> 00:34:19,791
about his lordship's actions
 or thoughts,

787
00:34:19,791 --> 00:34:21,192
no matter what I was thinking.

788
00:34:21,192 --> 00:34:24,028
Perhaps keep it
 to yourself, Bates.

789
00:34:24,028 --> 00:34:25,730
Of course, m'lord.

790
00:34:25,730 --> 00:34:27,098
Good man.

791
00:34:27,098 --> 00:34:28,433
It's almost like confession.

792
00:34:28,433 --> 00:34:30,234
It's almost like a Catholic
 thing where, you know,

793
00:34:30,234 --> 00:34:32,336
it's a sanctuary.

794
00:34:32,336 --> 00:34:34,772
The trouble is
 being dressed and undressed

795
00:34:34,772 --> 00:34:36,641
is an intimate business.

796
00:34:36,641 --> 00:34:39,210
We've forgiven Thomas
 his early sins, I know,

797
00:34:39,210 --> 00:34:41,512
but I cannot imagine I would
 ever quite feel the trust.

798
00:34:41,512 --> 00:34:43,114
Say no more, m'lord.

799
00:34:43,114 --> 00:34:44,749
I'm sure Mr. Bates
 will be home soon,

800
00:34:44,749 --> 00:34:46,150
which will settle
 the matter.

801
00:34:46,150 --> 00:34:48,352
COYLE:
 It's an extraordinary thing.

802
00:34:48,352 --> 00:34:51,322
On the one hand,
 there's a decorum there.

803
00:34:51,322 --> 00:34:53,991
There are boundaries
 in terms of what can be said

804
00:34:53,991 --> 00:34:55,993
and what can't be said,
 while at the same time,

805
00:34:55,993 --> 00:34:57,995
you're putting
 a man's trousers on.

806
00:34:57,995 --> 00:35:00,631
BONNEVILLE:
 Bates knows everything
 about my personal life,

807
00:35:00,631 --> 00:35:02,867
as indeed Anna knows
 everything about Mary.

808
00:35:02,867 --> 00:35:04,669
So it's a very one-sided
 relationship.

809
00:35:04,669 --> 00:35:05,703
How was dinner?

810
00:35:05,703 --> 00:35:06,704
Uphill.

811
00:35:06,704 --> 00:35:09,307
I'm so bored of Mr. Blake's
 cold shoulder.

812
00:35:09,307 --> 00:35:12,143
He hasn't warmed up, then?

813
00:35:12,143 --> 00:35:14,679
Lady Mary and Anna do have
 a very close relationship,

814
00:35:14,679 --> 00:35:17,782
and I would say
 they were friends as well.

815
00:35:17,782 --> 00:35:22,320
But they are still, you know,
 a servant and master.

816
00:35:22,320 --> 00:35:25,389
According to Mr. Napier,
 he finds me aloof.

817
00:35:25,389 --> 00:35:27,558
I'm not aloof, am I?

818
00:35:27,558 --> 00:35:29,527
Do you want me
 to answer truthfully

819
00:35:29,527 --> 00:35:31,129
or like a lady's maid?

820
00:35:31,129 --> 00:35:34,065
Let's move on.

821
00:35:34,065 --> 00:35:36,834
DOCKERY:
 Anna is always aware
 of her position,

822
00:35:36,834 --> 00:35:39,770
whereas Mary
 sometimes forgets.

823
00:35:39,770 --> 00:35:42,373
She forgets herself
 and she forgets Anna's position,

824
00:35:42,373 --> 00:35:46,911
so she'll tell her much more
 than she needs to.

825
00:35:46,911 --> 00:35:52,483
BRUCE:
 The intimacy of these
 relationships was inevitable.

826
00:35:52,483 --> 00:35:55,253
They spent so much time together

827
00:35:55,253 --> 00:35:57,388
endlessly dressing
 and undressing.

828
00:36:03,528 --> 00:36:05,530
Clothes mattered
 to the Edwardians

829
00:36:05,530 --> 00:36:08,099
because every detail
 meant something.

830
00:36:08,099 --> 00:36:10,968
The aristocracy
 wore impractical,

831
00:36:10,968 --> 00:36:14,705
high-maintenance clothing
 that proclaimed privilege.

832
00:36:14,705 --> 00:36:17,275
It sort of said,
 "I don't have to do any work

833
00:36:17,275 --> 00:36:20,278
and I have help
 putting this on."

834
00:36:20,278 --> 00:36:22,980
A great deal of effort
 went into making sure

835
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:26,951
that ladies and gentlemen
 appeared effortless.

836
00:36:26,951 --> 00:36:28,386
And why?

837
00:36:28,386 --> 00:36:30,821
Because it said,
 "We're in charge."

838
00:36:30,821 --> 00:36:32,757
Edith, what are you
 thinking?

839
00:36:32,757 --> 00:36:35,426
You know, I don't dislike him
 as much as you do.

840
00:36:35,426 --> 00:36:37,495
Perhaps you don't
 dislike him at all.

841
00:36:37,495 --> 00:36:38,996
Perhaps I don't.

842
00:36:40,798 --> 00:36:43,000
BRUCE:
 Ladies' dress was
 extravagantly elaborate

843
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:44,368
and guided by myriad rules.

844
00:36:46,704 --> 00:36:48,372
CARMICHAEL:
 I'm not allowed
 to wear a tiara

845
00:36:48,372 --> 00:36:49,640
because...

846
00:36:49,640 --> 00:36:51,709
You only wear a tiara
 if you're married.

847
00:36:55,313 --> 00:36:57,448
(laughing): Not sure
 about the rules on pearls.

848
00:36:57,448 --> 00:36:59,884
Only that I don't get
 to wear any.

849
00:36:59,884 --> 00:37:01,152
You look beautiful.

850
00:37:01,152 --> 00:37:02,420
Thank you,
 Sybil darling.

851
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:04,188
JAMES:
 Then there's the whole thing
 of gloves--

852
00:37:04,188 --> 00:37:05,856
when you have your gloves on,
 when you have them off.

853
00:37:05,856 --> 00:37:07,158
Even in the drawing room.

854
00:37:07,158 --> 00:37:08,726
How long are they
 going to stay?

855
00:37:08,726 --> 00:37:11,696
You have to leave your gloves on
 like pre- and post-dinner.

856
00:37:11,696 --> 00:37:13,464
We sort of have them
 all the time, you know?

857
00:37:13,464 --> 00:37:14,465
We have traveling gloves

858
00:37:14,465 --> 00:37:17,268
and dinner gloves
 and riding gloves.

859
00:37:23,641 --> 00:37:25,776
Is there anything more thrilling
 than a new frock?

860
00:37:25,776 --> 00:37:31,816
BRUCE:
 Such dress codes began centuries
 earlier in the sumptuary laws.

861
00:37:31,816 --> 00:37:34,285
These originally forbade anyone
 but the nobility

862
00:37:34,285 --> 00:37:37,388
from wearing things
 like fur or silk.

863
00:37:37,388 --> 00:37:39,457
On a usual day, you know,
 they'll dress for breakfast

864
00:37:39,457 --> 00:37:41,325
and then they'll maybe change

865
00:37:41,325 --> 00:37:43,427
if they go for a walk
 in the morning

866
00:37:43,427 --> 00:37:45,730
or go riding,
 then they'll change for lunch.

867
00:37:45,730 --> 00:37:47,498
Then they may change
 for the afternoon

868
00:37:47,498 --> 00:37:49,267
to sit and read
 or go and visit someone,

869
00:37:49,267 --> 00:37:51,736
and then they'll change again
 for dinner.

870
00:37:51,736 --> 00:37:53,070
Basically, these ladies

871
00:37:53,070 --> 00:37:55,640
just spend most
 of their time changing.

872
00:37:55,640 --> 00:37:59,343
