Robert understands,
 as an aristocrat,

993
00:42:33,751 --> 00:42:35,319
that he's in charge
 of everything.

994
00:42:35,319 --> 00:42:37,621
But Carson understands status

995
00:42:37,621 --> 00:42:39,223
and feels it
 more than anybody else

996
00:42:39,223 --> 00:42:40,824
because below stairs,

997
00:42:40,824 --> 00:42:42,927
it really counts where you are
 in the pecking order.

998
00:42:42,927 --> 00:42:47,298
Lord Grantham is in charge
 by virtue of his position.

999
00:42:47,298 --> 00:42:52,736
The aristocracy was expected
 to live off its land.

1000
00:42:52,736 --> 00:42:56,640
There was no question
 of actually having to find work.

1001
00:42:56,640 --> 00:42:57,908
I've got a job in Ripon.

1002
00:42:57,908 --> 00:42:59,143
I said I'll start tomorrow.

1003
00:42:59,143 --> 00:43:00,945
A job?

1004
00:43:08,052 --> 00:43:10,387
Making money
 and working nine to five

1005
00:43:10,387 --> 00:43:14,391
fills the minds of most of us,
 while yearning for the weekend.

1006
00:43:14,391 --> 00:43:16,961
But for the early
 20th century aristocrat,

1007
00:43:16,961 --> 00:43:19,663
this couldn't have been further
 from his thinking.

1008
00:43:19,663 --> 00:43:21,966
And what is a weekend?

1009
00:43:21,966 --> 00:43:23,801
BRUCE:
 Getting a job and making money

1010
00:43:23,801 --> 00:43:26,103
simply wasn't necessary.

1011
00:43:26,103 --> 00:43:28,238
You inherited your house
 and the land,

1012
00:43:28,238 --> 00:43:29,840
or you married one.

1013
00:43:29,840 --> 00:43:34,278
And then the land made you money
 and life was good.

1014
00:43:34,278 --> 00:43:37,481
You do not love
 the place yet?

1015
00:43:37,481 --> 00:43:39,383
Well, obviously, it's...

1016
00:43:39,383 --> 00:43:41,218
No, you don't love it.

1017
00:43:41,218 --> 00:43:43,454
You see a million bricks
 that may crumble,

1018
00:43:43,454 --> 00:43:44,855
a thousand gutters
 and pipes

1019
00:43:44,855 --> 00:43:46,857
that may block and leak,

1020
00:43:46,857 --> 00:43:48,726
and stone that will crack
 in the frost.

1021
00:43:48,726 --> 00:43:49,994
But you don't?

1022
00:43:51,829 --> 00:43:53,564
I see my life's work.

1023
00:43:55,766 --> 00:44:00,371
BRUCE:
 But their undisputed status
 as lords of the manor

1024
00:44:00,371 --> 00:44:03,407
was threatened by men
 with new money--

1025
00:44:03,407 --> 00:44:06,577
men like Sir Richard Carlisle--

1026
00:44:06,577 --> 00:44:08,846
ambitious men
 with pretentions to grandeur.

1027
00:44:08,846 --> 00:44:10,814
Hello.

1028
00:44:10,814 --> 00:44:13,250
We're so pleased to have
 you here, Sir Richard.

1029
00:44:13,250 --> 00:44:14,251
Lady Grantham.

1030
00:44:14,251 --> 00:44:15,252
Welcome.
 Thank you.

1031
00:44:15,252 --> 00:44:17,254
I hope the train
 wasn't too tiring.

1032
00:44:17,254 --> 00:44:20,624
Not a bit, no,
 I got a lot done.

1033
00:44:20,624 --> 00:44:23,794
New money was loud, strident
 and successful.

1034
00:44:23,794 --> 00:44:25,929
The aristocracy
 had to counter this

1035
00:44:25,929 --> 00:44:29,033
by labeling any mention
 of money as common.

1036
00:44:29,033 --> 00:44:31,135
The English upper classes
 never talk about money?

1037
00:44:31,135 --> 00:44:32,503
We don't like to.

1038
00:44:32,503 --> 00:44:34,304
But you don't mind
 thinking about it.

1039
00:44:36,006 --> 00:44:38,308
That's like the rich who say
 that money doesn't matter.

1040
00:44:38,308 --> 00:44:40,344
It matters enough
 when you haven't got it.

1041
00:44:40,344 --> 00:44:43,047
I know you don't care
 about our silly rules.

1042
00:44:43,047 --> 00:44:45,249
You're always very clear
 on that score.

1043
00:44:45,249 --> 00:44:47,785
You make me sound rude.

1044
00:44:47,785 --> 00:44:52,823
But I'm not ashamed of being
 what they call a self-made man.

1045
00:44:52,823 --> 00:44:54,458
BONNEVILLE:
 I think there's a very
 interesting difference

1046
00:44:54,458 --> 00:44:58,262
between someone like Carlisle
 in series two and Robert.

1047
00:44:58,262 --> 00:45:00,030
Carlisle comes
 from a meritocracy

1048
00:45:00,030 --> 00:45:02,633
based on, you know,
 the work you've done

1049
00:45:02,633 --> 00:45:04,368
and the finance you've earned.

1050
00:45:04,368 --> 00:45:07,204
His social status comes
 from the business he's set up.

1051
00:45:07,204 --> 00:45:09,807
Whereas, you know, Robert
 has been born into this society,

1052
00:45:09,807 --> 00:45:11,175
into this structure.

1053
00:45:11,175 --> 00:45:13,877
His sole purpose in life
 is to maintain the estate

1054
00:45:13,877 --> 00:45:15,612
and to hand it on
 in as good a nick

1055
00:45:15,612 --> 00:45:17,147
as he possibly can
 to the next generation.

1056
00:45:17,147 --> 00:45:19,850
BRUCE:
 But the certainty
 of landed wealth,

1057
00:45:19,850 --> 00:45:22,453
which the aristocracy
 had enjoyed for centuries,

1058
00:45:22,453 --> 00:45:24,822
was starting to fail.

1059
00:45:24,822 --> 00:45:27,591
Grain supplies
 from North America

1060
00:45:27,591 --> 00:45:30,561
drove down the value of crops,

1061
00:45:30,561 --> 00:45:33,864
and tax reforms threatened
 inherited wealth.

1062
00:45:33,864 --> 00:45:36,066
ROBERT:
 My fortune is the work
 of others.

1063
00:45:36,066 --> 00:45:38,302
I am a custodian, my dear,
 not an owner.

1064
00:45:40,037 --> 00:45:43,607
I must strive to be worthy
 of the task I've been set.

1065
00:45:43,607 --> 00:45:44,842
BONNEVILLE:
 Robert's not a businessman.

1066
00:45:44,842 --> 00:45:46,977
He has a shrewd idea
 about how to treat people,

1067
00:45:46,977 --> 00:45:48,912
he has a great respect
 for other people,

1068
00:45:48,912 --> 00:45:51,248
but in terms of running
 a small economy,

1069
00:45:51,248 --> 00:45:52,850
no, he's not your man.

1070
00:45:52,850 --> 00:45:54,885
So that's been, you know,
 a lot of fun

1071
00:45:54,885 --> 00:45:56,920
playing with some
 of the problems

1072
00:45:56,920 --> 00:45:59,623
that the modern day presents
 to someone like Robert.

1073
00:45:59,623 --> 00:46:01,258
Why not tackle it
 gradually?

1074
00:46:01,258 --> 00:46:04,161
Perhaps buy some time
 by investing your capital.

1075
00:46:04,161 --> 00:46:06,930
I hear of schemes every day

1076
00:46:06,930 --> 00:46:09,500
that'll double whatever's put
 into them or triple it or more.

1077
00:46:09,500 --> 00:46:12,102
Many schemes offer high rewards,
 very few deliver them.

1078
00:46:12,102 --> 00:46:13,837
Well, there's a chap in America,
 what's his name--

1079
00:46:13,837 --> 00:46:15,005
Charles Ponzi--

1080
00:46:15,005 --> 00:46:17,207
who offers a huge return
 after 90 days.

1081
00:46:17,207 --> 00:46:18,809
Robert of course is the guy

1082
00:46:18,809 --> 00:46:20,644
who, you know, thinks
 Ponzi's a great guy,

1083
00:46:20,644 --> 00:46:22,045
or, you know, there's,

1084
00:46:22,045 --> 00:46:23,914
"Everybody's piling
 into the Canadian Railways,

1085
00:46:23,914 --> 00:46:24,915
let's do that."

1086
00:46:24,915 --> 00:46:27,584
Harry Stoke has gone in
 with a bundle.

1087
00:46:27,584 --> 00:46:29,052
Then Harry Stoke,
 whoever he is, is a fool.

1088
00:46:29,052 --> 00:46:30,087
But it I could find out...

1089
00:46:30,087 --> 00:46:31,488
Robert, the last time you took

1090
00:46:31,488 --> 00:46:33,056
an interest in investment,
 you ruined the family!

1091
00:46:33,056 --> 00:46:35,092
Robert, who appears at times
 to be a dinosaur,

1092
00:46:35,092 --> 00:46:38,262
and sometimes is, but he's
 actually trying to conserve

1093
00:46:38,262 --> 00:46:39,997
the best of the old and the new,

1094
00:46:39,997 --> 00:46:42,366
and to make sure that the estate
 continues to function

1095
00:46:42,366 --> 00:46:43,634
to the best of its ability.

1096
00:46:43,634 --> 00:46:48,672
BRUCE:
 The best of the old
 meant a job and a home for life

1097
00:46:48,672 --> 00:46:51,208
to servants like Molesley.

1098
00:46:51,208 --> 00:46:55,712
Only misbehavior or misfortune
 would cost you your position.

1099
00:46:55,712 --> 00:46:59,650
DOYLE:
 Molesley finds himself surplus
 to needs.

1100
00:46:59,650 --> 00:47:02,953
And you realize very quickly,
 as people nowadays realize,

1101
00:47:02,953 --> 00:47:05,455
actually, that you're only
 a couple of paychecks away

1102
00:47:05,455 --> 00:47:08,826
from, you know, desperation.

1103
00:47:08,826 --> 00:47:10,794
Hello, Mr. Molesley.

1104
00:47:13,430 --> 00:47:14,965
How are things?

1105
00:47:14,965 --> 00:47:16,834
Well, as you can see,
 not very good.

1106
00:47:16,834 --> 00:47:18,101
I don't agree.

1107
00:47:18,101 --> 00:47:19,469
It's skilled work.

1108
00:47:19,469 --> 00:47:21,305
No, it isn't,
 not what I'm doing.

1109
00:47:21,305 --> 00:47:25,209
I'm sure if you just wait,
 something better will turn up.

1110
00:47:25,209 --> 00:47:27,811
I have waited
 and nothing's turned up.

1111
00:47:27,811 --> 00:47:29,713
I haven't earned a penny
 since Mr. Crawley died.

1112
00:47:29,713 --> 00:47:31,215
Now I owe money
 all over the village.

1113
00:47:31,215 --> 00:47:33,217
Yes, but surely
 with your skill...

1114
00:47:33,217 --> 00:47:34,384
Don't you understand

1115
00:47:34,384 --> 00:47:35,686
I'm at my wits' end?

1116
00:47:35,686 --> 00:47:37,187
FROGGATT:
 Money wasn't a subject

1117
00:47:37,187 --> 00:47:39,690
that was spoken about
 in polite conversation,

1118
00:47:39,690 --> 00:47:43,493
nor were intense emotions
 either.

1119
00:47:43,493 --> 00:47:45,195
So it's quite shocking for Anna.

1120
00:47:45,195 --> 00:47:47,197
I apologize,
 I should not have said that.

1121
00:47:47,197 --> 00:47:49,633
It was vulgar
 and self-important.

1122
00:47:49,633 --> 00:47:51,168
Please forgive me.

1123
00:47:52,703 --> 00:47:55,339
BONNEVILLE:
 There is no social welfare
 system,

1124
00:47:55,339 --> 00:47:57,741
there is no healthcare system,

1125
00:47:57,741 --> 00:48:00,611
and the people
 who work on your estate

1126
00:48:00,611 --> 00:48:03,680
live or die by your hand,
 really,

1127
00:48:03,680 --> 00:48:05,382
by the way you treat them.

1128
00:48:05,382 --> 00:48:08,452
BRUCE:
 On set, our work
 is just as elaborate

1129
00:48:08,452 --> 00:48:13,390
as the servants' work was
 for a great Edwardian family.

1130
00:48:13,390 --> 00:48:14,758
Where's the other rake?

1131
00:48:14,758 --> 00:48:17,094
I think you've probably got
 quite a lot in there.

1132
00:48:17,094 --> 00:48:18,929
It's just,
 I love gravel.

1133
00:48:18,929 --> 00:48:21,231
To me, gravel makes
 all the difference.

1134
00:48:21,231 --> 00:48:22,900
Do you notice how
 with the raking,

1135
00:48:22,900 --> 00:48:24,434
it looks completely different,

1136
00:48:24,434 --> 00:48:27,337
and with all these old houses,
 the gravel would have been raked

1137
00:48:27,337 --> 00:48:29,907
first thing in the morning
 every single day,

1138
00:48:29,907 --> 00:48:32,476
and if necessary,
 later on too.

1139
00:48:32,476 --> 00:48:34,211
BONNEVILLE:
 It goes back to this thing,

1140
00:48:34,211 --> 00:48:36,713
this analogy I've used
 in the past

1141
00:48:36,713 --> 00:48:38,849
of the estate really being
 a whole load of cogs

1142
00:48:38,849 --> 00:48:40,918
that service the one machine.

1143
00:48:40,918 --> 00:48:43,020
And if you take out a cog
 because you think

1144
00:48:43,020 --> 00:48:46,423
it's either, you know,
 not gonna work properly

1145
00:48:46,423 --> 00:48:48,859
or simply because you can't be
 bothered to oil it or something,

1146
00:48:48,859 --> 00:48:51,094
then the whole machine
 starts to break down.

1147
00:48:51,094 --> 00:48:53,297
BRUCE:
 Can you make
 a tighter line?

1148
00:48:53,297 --> 00:48:54,932
There.

1149
00:48:54,932 --> 00:48:58,001
There, hands by your side.

1150
00:48:58,001 --> 00:49:01,772
Knowing your place
 was the first rule and the last.

1151
00:49:01,772 --> 00:49:04,841
It's the point
 from which all manners sprang.

1152
00:49:04,841 --> 00:49:06,810
But it wasn't just
 the lower orders

1153
00:49:06,810 --> 00:49:08,779
who had to know
 where they belonged;

1154
00:49:08,779 --> 00:49:11,048
the aristocrats
 had to get it right too.

1155
00:49:11,048 --> 00:49:13,150
I've been meaning to speak
 to you about Molesley.

1156
00:49:13,150 --> 00:49:14,451
Oh?

1157
00:49:14,451 --> 00:49:16,553
Would you find me
 very ungrateful

1158
00:49:16,553 --> 00:49:19,056
if I dispensed
 with his services?

1159
00:49:19,056 --> 00:49:22,059
Why? Has he displeased you
 in some way?

1160
00:49:22,059 --> 00:49:23,193
Not at all.

1161
00:49:23,193 --> 00:49:24,594
It's simply that
 he's superfluous

1162
00:49:24,594 --> 00:49:26,096
to our style of living.

1163
00:49:26,096 --> 00:49:27,698
Is that quite fair,

1164
00:49:27,698 --> 00:49:29,333
to deprive a man
 of his livelihood

1165
00:49:29,333 --> 00:49:30,767
when he's done
 nothing wrong?

1166
00:49:30,767 --> 00:49:32,102
Well, I wouldn't
 quite put it...

1167
00:49:32,102 --> 00:49:34,271
Your mother derives satisfaction
 from her work at the hospital,

1168
00:49:34,271 --> 00:49:36,239
I think,
 some sense of self-worth.

1169
00:49:36,239 --> 00:49:37,607
Well, certainly.

1170
00:49:37,607 --> 00:49:39,409
Would you really deny the same
 to poor old Molesley?

1171
00:49:39,409 --> 00:49:41,845
And when you
 are master here,

1172
00:49:41,845 --> 00:49:44,481
is the butler to be dismissed,
 or the footman?

1173
00:49:44,481 --> 00:49:47,150
How many maids or kitchen staff
 will be allowed to stay,

1174
00:49:47,150 --> 00:49:48,885
or must every one be driven out?

1175
00:49:50,854 --> 00:49:54,224
We all have different parts
 to play, Matthew,

1176
00:49:54,224 --> 00:49:56,360
and we must all be allowed
 to play them.

1177
00:50:03,633 --> 00:50:07,404
BRUCE:
 The key to the aristocrat's
 view of the world

1178
00:50:07,404 --> 00:50:10,140
was not privilege;
 it was duty.

1179
00:50:13,844 --> 00:50:17,247
The point of an aristocrat
 was to be of use

1180
00:50:17,247 --> 00:50:19,750
to the land he owned
 and its people.

1181
00:50:19,750 --> 00:50:23,754
Robert owns everything
 he can see-- all this.

1182
00:50:23,754 --> 00:50:29,192
It's a kingdom of 6,000 acres,
 a miniature economy

1183
00:50:29,192 --> 00:50:33,530
that depends on his lordship's
 successful reign.

1184
00:50:33,530 --> 00:50:35,332
MATTHEW:
 I don't know
 about the rest of you,

1185
00:50:35,332 --> 00:50:37,234
but I sometimes think it's time
 we lived in a simpler way.

1186
00:50:37,234 --> 00:50:38,635
VIOLET:
 Oh, don't say that.

1187
00:50:38,635 --> 00:50:40,670
It's our job
 to provide employment.

1188
00:50:40,670 --> 00:50:43,273
An aristocrat
 with no servants

1189
00:50:43,273 --> 00:50:46,076
is as much use to the county
 as a glass hammer.

1190
00:50:46,076 --> 00:50:47,110
(laughs)

1191
00:50:52,549 --> 00:50:54,751
It's not just a rich man
 in his big castle

1192
00:50:54,751 --> 00:50:56,620
served by the lower orders.

1193
00:50:56,620 --> 00:50:59,689
Grantham serves them
 by keeping all this going

1194
00:50:59,689 --> 00:51:02,359
and by giving each
 a respectable place

1195
00:51:02,359 --> 00:51:05,629
in the mighty machine
 that was Great Britain.

1196
00:51:05,629 --> 00:51:07,931
It was the perfect balance

1197
00:51:07,931 --> 00:51:10,500
between privilege
 and responsibility.

1198
00:51:18,408 --> 00:51:19,576
CREW:
 Action!

1199
00:51:24,614 --> 00:51:26,349
Hold it there,
 thank you.

1200
00:51:27,584 --> 00:51:30,153
The thing that strikes me,
 as I struggle to make sure

1201
00:51:30,153 --> 00:51:33,123
everyone stands up straight
 and avoids hugging,

1202
00:51:33,123 --> 00:51:37,694
is that what we regard
 as strange and formal

1203
00:51:37,694 --> 00:51:41,331
to them was as natural
 as the air they breathed.

1204
00:51:41,331 --> 00:51:44,901
The real secret behind
 the manners of Downton Abbey

1205
00:51:44,901 --> 00:51:47,771
and the thing we all work
 so hard to get right

1206
00:51:47,771 --> 00:51:51,808
is that for them,
 this was effortless.

